## Category B: Layout & Spatial Hierarchy

**4. Increase the maximum container width from 1360px to 1440px.**
Line 45: `.block-container { max-width:1360px; }`. On a 2560×1440 display (standard for data professionals), this leaves 560px of unused space on each side. At 1440px, tables and charts have more room without looking stretched.

*Reasoning:* Data-dense applications benefit from horizontal space. OVERWATCH is a data app. The current cap wastes the widest monitors.

**5. Add a visual "zone" system — hero zone (KPIs), work zone (tables/charts), meta zone (captions).**
Currently every element gets the same spacing (`gap:0.55rem`). But KPIs should have more breathing room than data tables. Define 3 density zones: hero (more space), work (current), meta (compact). Apply automatically based on component type.

*Reasoning:* Uniform spacing creates visual monotony. Variable spacing creates hierarchy — the eye naturally lands on the items with more surrounding space.

**6. The sidebar active-page indicator (inset box-shadow) is too subtle.**
Line 243: `box-shadow:inset 3px 0 0 var(--ow-accent)`. On a dark sidebar, a 3px blue inset is easy to miss. Increase to 4px and add a subtle background tint: `background:linear-gradient(90deg,rgba(96,165,250,0.12),transparent)`.

*Reasoning:* Navigation orientation depends on knowing WHERE you are. The active indicator must be immediately visible in peripheral vision — a 3px line isn't sufficient.

**7. Add a "page transition" animation between sections.**
Line 254: stale elements get `opacity:0` on section switch. But the NEW section just appears (no enter transition). Add: `.element-container { animation: ow-appear 100ms ease-out; }` with a subtle translateY(3px) origin.

*Reasoning:* Instantaneous content replacement feels abrupt. A 100ms fade-in feels intentional. The difference between "the page glitched" and "the content updated" is one animation.

