## Category F: Drilldowns & Navigation

**19. Every table should support "click a row → navigate to detail" consistently.**
Some tables use `selectable_table` (row click → detail). Others use `styled_table` (no click). The inconsistency teaches users to NOT click rows (because sometimes nothing happens). Make all data tables clickable, even if the action is just expanding an inline detail below the row.

*Reasoning:* Consistent affordances build muscle memory. If SOME tables are clickable, users eventually stop trying on the ones that aren't — missing navigation opportunities.

**20. Add breadcrumbs showing the drill path.**
When a user navigates: Overview → Cost & Contract > Optimization > WH_ALFA_QUERY detail — there's no visual trail of where they are. Add: `OVERWATCH > Cost & Contract > Optimization > WH_ALFA_QUERY` at the top of the detail view.

*Reasoning:* Deep drilldowns without breadcrumbs create "where am I?" disorientation. The user loses context and can't easily back up one level.

**21. Cross-page navigation should preserve the source page as a "back" link.**
`request_navigation("Operations", "Queries")` sends the user there. But there's no "← Back to Cost & Contract" button at the destination. Store the source and render a back affordance.

*Reasoning:* Without "back," cross-page links feel like losing your place. Users avoid them because they don't want to lose context. A back link makes exploration safe.

**22. Entity 360 should be accessible FROM any table that mentions a warehouse, user, or database.**
Currently Entity 360 lives on Control Room. But every table on every page contains entity names. A warehouse name in the Cost table should be a link to "investigate this warehouse" (Entity 360 with that entity pre-filled).

*Reasoning:* The investigation tool (Entity 360) is only reachable from one page. The need to investigate arises from EVERY page. The tool should be universally accessible from any entity mention.

**23. Add "drill into time" — clicking a chart date point should filter all tables to that date.**
Click August 12 on the spend chart → all tables below filter to August 12's data. This is the "temporal drill" pattern that makes charts interactive rather than decorative.

*Reasoning:* A spike on August 12 makes the user think "what happened that day?" Currently they have to manually set a date filter. Chart clicks should do it automatically.

