## Category G: Flow & Progressive Disclosure

**24. Pages should have a "page-level verdict" — one semantic sentence before any content.**
Every page opens with a `page_header`. But the header is static ("Cost & Contract: Where the money goes..."). Add a DYNAMIC line below it: "You're 8% over budget pace. Top action: Tighten WH_ALFA_QUERY auto-suspend ($200/month)." This changes on every visit.

*Reasoning:* A static description tells you what the page IS. A dynamic verdict tells you what the page MEANS TODAY. The latter drives action without requiring the user to read anything else.

**25. The 6-8 section pill bar wraps poorly on 1280px laptops.**
At 1280px (13" MacBook), 6+ pills with labels like "Incidents & triage" or "Freshness & replay" overflow and wrap to two lines, consuming 60px+ of vertical space above the fold. Shorten all labels to ≤12 characters or use icons alongside abbreviated text.

*Reasoning:* The section nav is a fixed cost on every page. On narrow screens, it pushes content below the fold. Compact labels preserve vertical real estate.

**26. Provide a "skip to content" affordance when the filter toolbar is visible.**
Some pages show a filter toolbar (company, days, warehouse, database, schema) before any data. On first visit these are necessary. On the 50th visit, they're 60px of noise above the fold. Add a "collapse filters" toggle that remembers preference.

*Reasoning:* Power users have stable filters. Showing the toolbar every time is chrome noise for them. Collapsing it (with a tiny expand button) gives back the fold.

**27. The `lazy_sections` pattern should show a BADGE with item count or status per section.**
Line 96 in components: `lazy_sections` renders pill labels. But the label "Alerts" doesn't tell you if there are 0 or 50 open alerts without clicking. Show: "Alerts (7)" or "Tasks ⚠" to preview section content from the nav bar.

*Reasoning:* Section badges eliminate "exploration clicks" — the user knows which section needs attention without opening each one.

