## Category I: Loading States & Performance Perception

**31. Show skeleton loaders instead of blank space during data fetch.**
When a section loads, there's a brief empty space before content appears. Render a ghosted card placeholder (gray rectangles mimicking the expected layout) that gets replaced by real content.

*Reasoning:* 300ms of blank space feels broken. 300ms of a skeleton feels like "data is loading." The perceived performance improves even if actual load time is identical.

**32. Show "query in progress" with elapsed time for slow reads.**
Some ACCOUNT_USAGE reads take 5-15 seconds. During that time, nothing is visible. Add a subtle indicator: "Loading query families... (4.2s)" so the user knows the app isn't frozen.

*Reasoning:* An unresponsive interface for 10 seconds creates anxiety ("is it broken?"). A loading indicator with elapsed time sets expectations ("it's working, just slow").

**33. The page should NOT fully re-render when changing a filter that only affects one section.**
Streamlit reruns everything on any widget change. But `lazy_sections` means only one section renders at a time. If only the visible section's data depends on the changed filter, the other sections shouldn't re-fetch. Your caching handles this, but the visual re-render of the section nav and header still flickers.

*Reasoning:* Visual stability during interaction builds confidence. Flickering on every filter change undermines the feeling of solidity.

