## Category K: Micro-Interactions & Polish

**37. Add hover states that reveal actions on table rows.**
Most tables are static. On hover, rows should subtly highlight AND reveal contextual actions: "Drill →" or "Add to watchlist" or "Investigate." This is the "actions-on-hover" pattern from every modern data tool.

*Reasoning:* Discoverable actions increase feature usage. A user who doesn't know they can drill into a warehouse will never drill into a warehouse. A hover hint teaches them.

**38. The brand dot animation (pulse) should be conditional on platform health.**
Line 184: `.ow-brand-dot` pulses on a 2.8s cycle unconditionally. Make it status-aware: pulsing = healthy (all systems green). Solid = degraded. Rapid pulse = critical. The heartbeat becomes a system health indicator.

*Reasoning:* The brand dot is visible on every page (sidebar). If it encodes health, it becomes a persistent ambient status indicator — the user learns the system's state without looking at any metric.

**39. Add subtle transitions on section content entry.**
Line 254 hides stale content. But new content has no entrance animation. Add: `@keyframes ow-enter { from { opacity:0; transform:translateY(4px) } to { opacity:1; transform:none } }` with 80ms duration on incoming elements.

*Reasoning:* Enter animations communicate causality ("I clicked, this appeared"). Without them, content changes feel instantaneous and disconnected from the user's action.

**40. The KPI card hover effect (shadow2) should also show a subtle border glow on the severity stripe.**
Line 65: cards hover with enhanced shadow. But the severity stripe (the most important visual signal) doesn't change on hover. On hover, intensify the stripe: `opacity:0.85 → opacity:1; width:3px → width:4px` with transition.

*Reasoning:* Hover effects should reinforce the card's identity. The stripe IS the card's semantic identity. Making it react to hover emphasizes what matters most about the card.

